{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"**Answer**\n\n- Added a **`codex-sandboxed`** lane to `run.sh`.\n- Implemented `codex_sandboxed_run()`:\n  - Runs Codex inside `sandbox/agent.sb` just like the `kimi` lane.\n  - Uses a temporary fake `HOME` (`/tmp/khome-XXXXXX`) so the agent cannot touch the real home.\n  - Writes Codex’s final output to a scratch directory (`/tmp/codex-sb-XXXXXX`) outside the sandbox.\n  - After the sandbox finishes, copies `final.txt` from the scratch location back into the run folder.\n  - Cleans up both the fake home and scratch directories.\n- Updated the lane selector in `run.sh` to handle the new `codex-sandboxed` option.\n- Updated the lane description comment at the top of `run.sh`.\n\n**Parts of the colleague’s note that were applied**\n\n- Reused the `sandbox-exec -f sandbox/agent.sb` pattern from the `kimi` lane.\n- Prevented reads/writes to the real `$HOME` using `-D HOME=` and a fake HOME directory.\n- Created a temporary scratch folder in `/tmp` for Codex’s `-o` output, avoiding sandbox‑denied writes.\n- Copied the output file out of the sandbox after execution.\n- Cleaned up temporary directories after the run.\n\nThe other details (specific error messages, handling of other agents) were not needed for this task. Running `./run.sh <task> codex-sandboxed` now executes Codex in a sandboxed environment while still producing the expected artifacts in the run folder."}}
